  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Islamic Glass in the Making shows clear early scholarly and usage uptake: it has 2 OpenAlex citations, 1,965 recorded downloads, and a small but focused citation trail in archaeometric glass research. The record also links the book to European Research Council support and to the SDG theme of Peace, Justice and strong institutions.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Nadine Schibille\u2019s Islamic Glass in the Making is an archaeologically grounded study of the transition to plant-ash glass production in the Islamic world. Its impact profile is still early, but the available evidence already points to active use in specialist research communities and to strong reader interest. The book has been downloaded 1,965 times through OAPEN, suggesting sustained engagement with the full text rather than only passing visibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is modest but concentrated: OpenAlex records 2 citations, both appearing in 2024 and 2025 articles in Archaeological and Anthropological Sciences. Those citing works focus on cultural heritage materials analysis, building materials and conservation, and conservation techniques, indicating that the book is being used as a reference point in technical studies of glass composition, workshop practice, and provenance. This is an early citation signal rather than a mature one, but it is specific and disciplinary in character.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book also shows reach through open-access infrastructure. OpenAIRE lists multiple open instances of the work, and the record identifies the title as hybrid open access. That accessibility likely supports the download activity and lowers barriers for researchers, students, and museum or conservation audiences working with glass materials and historical production methods.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding metadata also places the book in a policy-relevant frame. Its SDG alignment with Peace, Justice and strong institutions reflects the broader value of preserving and interpreting cultural heritage, while the European Research Council funding record signals research backing at a competitive level. Taken together, the evidence suggests a work that is already being used in specialist scholarship, is being read beyond the author\u2019s immediate network, and has a foundation for further influence as the citation record develops.</p>",
